#import "template.typ": *

#let metadata = (
  title: "Right Seat Conversion",
  unit: "Instructor Training | Element 2.4 / 9.1 — Right Seat Competency",
  author: "C.Moroney",
  version: "v1.0.0",
  plan_type: "Instructor Training",
)

#let lesson = (
title: [#metadata.title]

,aim: [
Operate and control the aircraft competently from the right seat, adapting to the changed sight picture and parallax, with steep turns and circuits (including emergencies) as the primary vehicle for demonstrating that competency.
]

,overview: [
*Booking exception*: This lesson uses a 2-hour slot, not the standard 4-hour block — a 20-minute briefing followed by a 1-hour flight.

Following PMI completion, the Instructor Candidate (IC) converts to flying from the right (instructor's) seat before beginning the syllabus walkthrough. The primary challenge is not new manoeuvres but re-calibrating a changed sight picture, parallax on the primary flight instruments, and reference points that were learned from the left seat. Steep turns (attitude/reference-point re-calibration) and circuits including emergencies (spacing/judgement re-calibration under the highest workload) make up the bulk of the flight. This maps to Instructor Training Syllabus Element 2.4 (right seat/instructor seat competency) and Element 9.1 (instructor seat conversion).
]

,learning_outcomes: [
#text(size:12pt)[*Knowledge*]
- Explain how parallax affects reading analog instruments on the pilot side from an oblique viewing angle
- Explain why side-by-side seating changes perceived aircraft attitude and reference points relative to the nose, independent of eye height
- Recall that downwind spacing judgement is largely unaffected by seat position, but that the *perspective* on final/base and steep turn reference points is materially different from the right seat
#text(size:12pt)[*Skills*]
- Taxi, take off, and land the aircraft from the right seat to normal ab-initio tolerances
- Fly steep turns (60° AoB) from the right seat, re-establishing correct nose/horizon reference points
- Fly a full normal circuit from the right seat to instructor-standard tolerances
- Manage at least one circuit emergency (simulated engine/system failure) from the right seat, maintaining safe aircraft management while adapting to the new sight picture
#text(size:12pt)[*Non-Technical Skills*]
- Maintain effective lookout despite the unfamiliar seating position [SA]
- Verbalise the sight-picture adjustment explicitly, as this same adjustment will need to be taught to future students [HF]
- Clear, unambiguous control handover/takeover phrasing [CRM]
]

,theory_knowledge: [
- CAA Flight Instructor Guide — parallax error section (p.55) and right-hand-seat circuit perspective section (p.114)
- RAAus FOM Section 2.15, Para 2.4 — Aircraft group and type limitations, right seat/instructor seat competency requirement
]

,ground_brief_topics: [
Total time: 20 minutes \
Equipment required: Aircraft model, access to seating diagram/photos of both seat positions if available \

*Non-Technical Skills* (5 minutes) [HF] [SA]
- Anticipate temporary disorientation from the seat change; verbalise the adjustment as it happens — this becomes teaching material later
- Lookout discipline unchanged despite altered visual references

*Parallax and Sight Picture* (10 minutes)
- Parallax on pilot side analog instruments from an oblique angle
- Nose/horizon reference points will look different for the same attitude — the instrument readings and the aircraft's actual behaviour have not changed, only the view of them
- Circuit spacing judgement (downwind) is not materially affected by seat position or eye height; final/base perspective and steep turn references are

*Steep Turns and Circuit Reference Points* (5 minutes)
- New nose-attitude reference for turns from the right seat
- New sightline for the extended centreline, base leg turn point, and round-out/flare picture and centerline tracking
]

,ground_tasks:[
#text(size:12pt)[*Instructor Trainer (IT)*]
- Conduct preflight and seat/harness setup from the left seat as PIC for this lesson
- Brief the control handover/takeover phrasing to be used
- Deliver the 20-minute brief above
#text(size:12pt)[*Instructor Candidate (IC)*]
- Complete IMSAFE self-assessment
- Confirm right-seat ergonomics: controls, instruments, and checklist all accessible and clearly visible from the new position
]

,in_flight_tasks:[
#text(size:12pt)[*Demonstration*]
- IT demonstrates one steep turn in both directions from the left seat first (as a reference the IC has already seen from that seat), then hands over
#text(size:12pt)[*IC Practice*]
- Taxi, take-off, and climb-out from the right seat
- Steep turns (up to 60° AoB) left and right, re-establishing the correct nose/horizon picture; IT calls out any drift from the ab-initio tolerance
- Full normal circuit(s) from the right seat to instructor-standard tolerances
- One simulated circuit emergency (e.g. simulated engine failure downwind or on climb-out) managed from the right seat — IT introduces the failure, IC manages the aircraft and communicates as PIC would
- Land from the right seat
]

,common_errors:[
- *Under-controlling near the ground*: New sight picture on round-out/flare often produces a late or shallow flare — brief this explicitly and be ready to assist early on first landings.
- *Consistenly right of centreline*: New sight picture tends to lead IC to track to the right of taxiway and runway centreline; point out drift from centreline.
- *Steep turn nose reference set from left-seat habit*: IC unconsciously uses the old reference point; correct by having them find the new reference deliberately, not by feel.
- *Base/final turn point misjudged*: Similar cause — talk through the new sightline before the turn, not during it.
- *Rudder "ghost inputs" during handover*: IT must consciously remove feet from pedals on handover, as per general dual-control handover practice — otherwise it undermines the IC's sense of control.
]

,post_flight:[
- *IC Self-Assessment*: IC describes which reference points needed the most conscious re-calibration.
- *IT Feedback* (5 min):
  - Evaluate against completion standards (S, C, NC) for right-seat handling.
  - Confirm the sight-picture adjustments have been verbalised clearly enough that the IC could explain them to a future student.
- *Next Steps*: Confirm readiness to begin the syllabus walkthrough from the right seat.
]

,standards_for_progression:[
- Taxi, take-off, and landing from the right seat to normal ab-initio tolerances
- Steep turns (60° AoB) within ±5° AoB / ±100 ft, right seat
- Full normal circuit within standard ab-initio tolerances, right seat
- Circuit emergency managed safely to a successful outcome with clear communication throughout
- IC can articulate, unprompted, how the sight picture differs from the left seat for at least steep turns and the base/final turn
- *Outcome*: Recorded in IC training record (NC/C, Instructor Training Syllabus Element 2.4 / 9.1). Full instructor-standard patter and demonstration quality from the right seat is confirmed progressively through the syllabus walkthrough, not required here.
]

,safety:[
- *Weather*: Horizon visible, ceiling >3000 ft, circuit conditions within normal ab-initio limits.
- *Altitude*: Steep turns above 1500 ft AGL; circuit work at normal circuit height.
- *Emergency Procedures*: Forced landing area pre-selected before any simulated failure; IT retains override authority throughout as PIC.
- *TEM*:
  - Brief and confirm control handover/takeover phrasing — critical given two experienced pilots aboard and a seat/role change.
  - Monitor for IC disorientation on first right-seat landings; be ready to take over early rather than late.
- *Aircraft Limits*: Avoid exceeding Vno or abrupt inputs per POH.
]

,homework:[
- Review the syllabus walkthrough lesson list and running order for the sessions to follow.
- Reflect: identify one further manoeuvre (beyond steep turns and circuits) where right-seat perspective is likely to matter, ahead of reaching it in the walkthrough.
]
)

#show: body => lesson_plan(metadata, lesson, body)
